A740 program - the target and its optimisation metric.
This is a decision record, not a patch. No Mesa source is changed by it.

Target
  Adreno 740 (Snapdragon 8 Gen 2, SM8550). Mesa name FD740, chip_id
  0x43050A01 (KGSL) / 0xFFFF43050A01 (no-speedbin fallback). Architecture is
  A7xx generation: CHIP.A7XX, props [a7xx_base, a7xx_gen2].

  Verified in src/freedreno/common/freedreno_devices.py at Mesa 4f554da
  (FD740 entry, a740_magic_regs, cs_shared_mem_size = 32 * 1024).

Why this metric
  Sustained performance, not peak FPS. The failure mode that matters is
  frame-rate collapse once the SoC reaches its thermal limit, which a short
  benchmark cannot see. The unit of improvement is energy per frame, because
  peak FPS and sustained FPS move in OPPOSITE directions when you overclock:
  raising the power ceiling raises the burst and lowers the plateau.

  Consequence recorded as an anti-goal: PWR_MAX-style power pinning and
  unconditional GMEM forcing are NOT to be enabled. The former guarantees the
  thermal wall; the latter breaks large render targets that do not fit in GMEM
  and can cost more energy than it saves.

  Also an anti-goal: tuning the A740 magic registers for performance. They are
  silicon-quirk workarounds in upstream Mesa (TPL1_DBG_ECO_CNTL,
  UCHE_CACHE_WAYS, SP_CHICKEN_BITS family), not tuning knobs.

Performance experiments - UNMEASURED, opt-in
  Two candidates from docs/A740_PROGRAM.md §4 are implemented as separate opt-in
  variants so they can be measured on real A740 hardware. Neither is a default
  and neither is claimed to be a win; each must clear the AGENTS.md §3 bar
  (hypothesis + 10+ min sustained run + device regression check) before promotion.

  patches/a740_gcm.py (variant a740-gcm)
    ir3_nir.c: debug_get_num_option("GCM", 0) -> 1, enabling nir_opt_gcm with
    hoisting. Hypothesis: fewer instructions executed per draw is the primary
    energy lever (A740_PROGRAM.md §1). Risk: hoisting raises register pressure,
    and the A740 has only 32 KiB cs_shared_mem_size, so occupancy is already
    capped - watch for spills. The GCM env var still overrides the default, so
    one binary can serve both arms of the test.

  patches/a740_suballoc.py (variant a740-suballoc)
    tu_device.cc: pipeline_suballoc and kgsl_profiling_suballoc 128 * 1024 ->
    512 * 1024. Hypothesis: fewer allocator round trips on the CPU side while the
    GPU waits is a steady-state win. Cost, from upstream's own comment in
    tu_suballoc.cc: "fragmentation can be an issue for default_size > PAGE_SIZE
    and small allocations", and this reserves 4x the memory. Measure MEMORY as
    well as FPS - rising reserved memory with flat FPS means it loses.
    autotune_suballoc and trace_suballoc are deliberately untouched.

  Both scripts are idempotent, assert their own result, and refuse to patch if the
  upstream anchor moved rather than writing something subtly wrong. The
  a740-gcm-suballoc variant runs both so the two effects can be separated.

  NOT wired into .github/workflows/turnip_build_combined.yml: these are
  experiment arms, not released drivers. The Makefile appends them to
  scripts_of(); scripts/lint.sh's three-way table check covers the four released
  variants only, which is why adding these did not require a matrix change.

Device identification (cosmetic)
Device identification (cosmetic)
  patches/a740_devname.py rewrites the Vulkan deviceName in tu_device.cc so this
  fork reports "Turnip (Banners A740)" instead of Mesa's default
  "Turnip Adreno (TM) 740". Upstream keys that string off the GPU id, so every
  A740 Turnip build reports it identically; Balemuni works around the same
  limitation by overriding the name too.

  Cosmetic only. Two consequences worth knowing:
    - disk_cache_create(device->name, ...) uses it for the shader-cache
      directory, so the first run after installing recompiles shaders once.
    - Nothing else keys off it: no behaviour, feature, or performance change.

  The AdrenoTools / BannerHub driver-list name is a separate field (meta.json
  "name"), set by META_NAME in the Makefile. The two are independent.

  Not part of the upstream &drivers matrix in turnip_build_combined.yml - the
  Makefile appends it to scripts_of() on top, so the variant -> patch mapping
  still matches CI. scripts/lint.sh asserts the two agree.

Reviewed for adoption: Balemuni/Balemunis-Aurora ("Apex Universal V2"), read
2026. Verdict per claim, checked against Mesa 4f554da:

  IR3 GCM=1        ADOPT as an experiment. Real: ir3_nir.c reads
                   debug_get_num_option("GCM", 0), default off; 1 enables
                   nir_opt_gcm with hoisting. Already reachable at runtime via
                   the env var, so no patch is needed to measure it.

  suballoc 128->512KB
                   ADOPT as an experiment. Real: tu_device.cc initialises
                   pipeline_suballoc and kgsl_profiling_suballoc at
                   128 * 1024. Note upstream already uses 512 * 1024 for
                   trace_suballoc, so the value is not arbitrary. Must be
                   measured; it trades memory for fewer allocator round trips.

  shader cache 4GB REJECT as a patch. Upstream does not hardcode 1 GB:
                   tu_device.cc calls disk_cache_create(..., 0) and
                   util/disk_cache.c reads MESA_SHADER_CACHE_MAX_SIZE from the
                   environment. It is a runtime setting. Patching a constant
                   would patch the wrong thing.

  Zelda TOTK/BOTW REJECT - already upstream. tu_dont_care_as_load and
  fixes             tu_allow_oob_indirect_ubo_loads are in
                   src/freedreno/vulkan/00-turnip-defaults.conf, scoped per
                   engine. Re-implementing them globally would lose that
                   scoping and could regress non-matching titles.

  Cortex-X3 tuning REJECT - not driver work. CPU instruction selection belongs to
                   the host app and the OS; Turnip cannot tune a CPU core.

Note on the source: that project publishes prebuilt ZIPs and a README, with no
patches or source. Nothing was copied from it. Its value was as a list of claims
to verify, recorded above so the next reader does not repeat the work.

Reviewed for adoption: WinNative-Emu/Drivers patches/apply_a7xx_gen2_ubwc_hint.py,
read 2026-10-04. Verdict: REJECT - already covered, and wrong for this hardware.

  The script appends GPUProps(enable_tp_ubwc_flag_hint = True) to the A7xx gen2
  props list of the FD740 / Adreno X1-85 add_gpus block - the identical edit that
  patches/8g2_oneui.py already makes.

  Duplicate         REJECT. Ran both scripts against a copy of this repo's Mesa
                    tree: the two produce a byte-identical freedreno_devices.py.
                    The X1-85 is the last GPUId in the FD740 block, so upstream's
                    anchor lands on the same props list. No new coverage.

  No firmware gate  REJECT - this is the substantive blocker. The hint must match
                    every driver on the device: Mesa leaves it off to match the
                    stock v6xx system driver, while newer One UI firmware ships a
                    driver that sets it, so Turnip has to match or the UI flickers.
                    It is firmware-dependent, not a silicon fix, which is why
                    patches/8g2_oneui.py is a separate opt-in variant. Upstream has
                    no such gate, so adding it to EXTRA_SCRIPT would enable the
                    hint for every A740 / X1-85 and CAUSE the glitch on non-One-UI
                    hardware (Ayaneo, etc.) - the exact regression AGENTS.md
                    section 6 warns about.

  Rationale wrong   Upstream's docstring says "A7xx_gen2 inherits a7xx_base,
                    which does not set enable_tp_ubwc_flag_hint." It does set it.
                    freedreno_devices.py, a7xx_gen2, upstream:
                        enable_tp_ubwc_flag_hint = False,
                    carrying the comment "Most devices with a740 have blob v6xx
                    which doesn't have this hint set. Match them for better
                    compatibility by default." That is an explicit upstream
                    decision to match the stock blob driver, not an omission.
                    Upstream Mesa also already ships the hint ON for two siblings
                    of the same generation - X1-45 / FD735 and FD740v3 (Quest 3) -
                    and off for FD740. The flag varies per part in-tree, which is
                    what confirms it is not a blanket A7xx-gen2 fix.

  Fails open        Upstream exits 0 with a bare WARNING when the anchor misses.
                    The fail-hard legs hash git diff around every EXTRA_SCRIPT
                    (build_turnip_wayland.sh, build_turnip_linux.sh) and would die
                    with a confusing "$s changed nothing", while the lenient
                    Android leg would silently ship without the fix.
                    patches/8g2_oneui.py exits FATAL instead, which is this repo's
                    convention.

  Symptom scope     Upstream describes vertical-line corruption, which is NOT the
                    closed black-box defect in docs/A740_BLACK_BOX.md - where this
                    same flag was built both ways on A740 hardware with no effect.
                    So it is not directly falsified by that record, but it is the
                    same flag and the same match-the-blob-driver mechanism, and the
                    8g2-oneui variant already expresses that answer with the
                    firmware gate the real fix needs.

  Keep patches/8g2_oneui.py as the single implementation. Nothing was copied from
  this script.